# 6. Database Tables Explained

IMPORTANT:

There are 2 categories:

| Category          | Meaning               |
| ----------------- | --------------------- |
| Definition Tables | Workflow template     |
| Runtime Tables    | Actual live execution |

---

# PART A – DEFINITION TABLES

These define HOW workflow SHOULD behave.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_DEFINITION ✅ Already Proposed

Master workflow template.

Example:
APR_REMEDIATION

Stores:

* workflow name
* workflow code
* version
* active flag

Why needed?
Reusable workflow templates.

Many CAPs can use same workflow.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_GROUP ✅ Already Proposed

Groups tasks into phases.

Examples:

* Population Processing
* Validation
* Approval

Why needed?
Without groups:

* workflow becomes flat
* UI becomes messy
* parallel execution difficult

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_DEFINITION ✅ Already Proposed

MOST IMPORTANT DEFINITION TABLE.

Defines each task.

Example Tasks:

* Fetch Population
* Validate Population
* Calculate APR
* Generate Letters

Stores:

* task name
* task type
* SLA duration
* mandatory flag

Why needed?
Defines actual workflow steps.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_GROUP_DEPENDENCY ✅ Already Proposed

Defines:
Which group starts after another group?

Example:
Validation starts after Population Processing.

Why needed?
Controls stage-level orchestration.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_DEPENDENCY ⭐ Suggested Addition

Why added?
Your proposal only had group dependency.

But real workflows need task-to-task dependency.

Example:
Validate Population depends on Fetch Population.

Why important?
Needed for:

* automation
* dynamic execution
* parallel workflows
* enterprise orchestration

---

## TABLE: SLA_POLICY ⭐ Suggested Addition

Why added?
Currently SLA inside task definition is too rigid.

Business changes SLA frequently.

Better Design:
Separate SLA from task definition.

Example:

* Task must complete in 24 hours
* Escalate after 48 hours

Why important?
Makes SLA configurable without changing workflow structure.

---

## TABLE: ASSIGNMENT_RULE ⭐ Suggested Addition

Why added?
Task assignment should not be hardcoded.

Example:

* Assign to CAP_OWNER
* Assign to COMPLIANCE_MANAGER

Why important?
Supports:

* dynamic routing
* future automation
* role-based assignment

---

# PART B – RUNTIME TABLES

These tables track WHAT is happening RIGHT NOW.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_INSTANCE ✅ Already Proposed

One running workflow.

Example:
CAP-1045 running APR workflow.

Stores:

* workflow status
* start time
* completion time

Why needed?
Main runtime parent entity.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_GROUP_INSTANCE ✅ Already Proposed

Running version of a group.

Example:
Validation Group for CAP-1045.

Why needed?
Tracks:

* group progress
* stage completion
* parallel branches

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE ✅ Already Proposed

MOST IMPORTANT RUNTIME TABLE.

One actual running task.

Example:
Validate Population for CAP-1045.

Stores:

* assignee
* status
* SLA deadline
* completion time
* escalation flag

Suggested Additional Fields:

* retry_count
* reminder_sent_count
* last_reminder_sent_at
* escalated

Why important?
Needed for:

* retries
* reminders
* escalation handling

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_TASK_AUDIT ✅ Already Proposed

Stores task history.

Example:

* READY → IN_PROGRESS
* IN_PROGRESS → COMPLETED

Why needed?
Enterprise audit/compliance.

---

## TABLE: WORKFLOW_EVENT ✅ Already Proposed

Stores system events.

Example Events:

* TASK_CREATED
* TASK_COMPLETED
* TASK_FAILED
* SLA_BREACHED

Suggested Additional Fields:

* correlation_id
* event_source
* causation_id

Why important?
Helps:

* debugging
* tracing
* Pub/Sub tracking

---

## TABLE: NOTIFICATION_QUEUE ⭐ Suggested Addition

Why added?
Emails should NOT be sent directly inside workflow transaction.

Flow:
Workflow Service
↓
creates notification row
↓
Notification Service sends email

Why important?
Supports:

* retries
* recovery
* async communication

---

# 7. Full Workflow Execution Flow

Example Workflow:

Fetch Population
↓
Validate Population
↓
APR Calculation
↓
Approval
↓
Generate Letters

---

## STEP 1 – User Creates CAP

API:
POST /caps

Flow:
UI
↓
Gateway
↓
Workflow Service
↓
Read WORKFLOW_DEFINITION
↓
Create WORKFLOW_INSTANCE
↓
Create TASK_GROUP_INSTANCE
↓
Create TASK_INSTANCE
↓
First task becomes READY

Tables Used:

* WORKFLOW_DEFINITION
* WORKFLOW_TASK_GROUP
* WORKFLOW_TASK_DEFINITION
* WORKFLOW_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_TASK_GROUP_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_EVENT
* WORKFLOW_TASK_AUDIT

---

## STEP 2 – User Opens Dashboard

APIs:

* GET /tasks/my-tasks
* GET /workflows/{workflowId}

Workflow Service Reads:

* WORKFLOW_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE

---

## STEP 3 – User Starts Task

API:
POST /tasks/{taskId}/start

Workflow Service Does:

* Validate state transition
* Update TASK_INSTANCE
* Write TASK_AUDIT
* Write WORKFLOW_EVENT

---

## STEP 4 – User Completes Task

API:
POST /tasks/{taskId}/complete

Workflow Service Flow:

* Update task status
* Write audit
* Write event
* Check dependency
* Activate next task

Tables Used:

* WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_TASK_AUDIT
* WORKFLOW_EVENT
* WORKFLOW_TASK_DEPENDENCY

---

## STEP 5 – Automated Task Starts

Suppose APR Calculation is automated.

Workflow Service Publishes:
TASK_REQUESTED

to Pub/Sub.

APR Service Consumes Event.

Runs:

* calculations
* queries
* processing

APR Service Publishes:
TASK_COMPLETED

Workflow Service Consumes Result.

Updates:
WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE

and activates next task.

---

## STEP 6 – SLA Monitoring

Scheduler Checks:
SLA_POLICY

If overdue:
Workflow service:

* mark SLA breached
* write event
* create notification

Tables Used:

* SLA_POLICY
* WORKFLOW_TASK_INSTANCE
* WORKFLOW_EVENT
* NOTIFICATION_QUEUE

---

## STEP 7 – Notification Sent

Notification Service Reads:
NOTIFICATION_QUEUE

Sends Email.

Then updates:
status = SENT

---

## STEP 8 – Workflow Completes

When all tasks completed:
WORKFLOW_INSTANCE.status = COMPLETED

Event Written:
WORKFLOW_COMPLETED

